The `ModuleLoader` class uses an unordered map to store loaded modules, ensuring efficient memory management and preventing leaks. It provides methods to load, unload, and retrieve modules, as well as initialize and cleanup all modules in the loader. The class is designed to handle high concurrency through thread safety using a mutex.

The code also includes a `ThirdPartyLibrary` class that inherits from the `Module` abstract base class. This example demonstrates how to create a third-party library module and integrate it with the `ModuleLoader`.

In the `main` function, an instance of the `ModuleLoader` is created, and a `ThirdPartyLibrary` module is loaded and initialized using the loader. The modules can be used as needed, and when done, all modules are cleaned up and unloaded properly.

This implementation provides a solid foundation for managing multiple third-party libraries and plugins within an application. Additional features such as performance optimization, logging level evaluation, and memory management strategies can be added based on specific requirements and use cases.']

